WEBVTT

1
00:00:01.169 --> 00:00:06.169
In this section, we're going to walk you through the hands-on station for Switchboard setup and launch.

2
00:00:06.169 --> 00:00:11.169
Now, before we get started in the engine actually getting our Switchboard configuration set up,

3
00:00:11.169 --> 00:00:14.169
I do want to point out that the first time you launch Switchboard,

4
00:00:14.169 --> 00:00:21.170
you will need to make sure that the Python dependencies do get added to every computer you are working from

5
00:00:21.170 --> 00:00:25.170
in order to synchronize everything using the Switchboard listener.

6
00:00:25.170 --> 00:00:30.170
Now, as we get ready to launch, there is something that you're going to need to be aware of.

7
00:00:30.170 --> 00:00:37.170
Because we're working from a stage instead of at home, you will need to enable the Perforce option in your Switchboard configuration.

8
00:00:37.170 --> 00:00:44.170
This is because you're going to need version control to synchronize each of the render nodes for your Switchboard configuration.

9
00:00:44.170 --> 00:00:52.170
Now, Perforce is the ideal version for control, but not a dependency if you're using other version control types or a non-network setup.

10
00:00:52.170 --> 00:00:57.170
Now, let's switch over to the engine so that we can set up our Switchboard configuration.

11
00:00:57.170 --> 00:01:03.390
Alright, we'll begin here by going up to our toolbar and you'll notice that I've got an option here for Launch Switchboard.

12
00:01:03.390 --> 00:01:11.390
I've also got some actions related to Switchboard here, including Launch Switchboard listener and Launch Switchboard listener on login.

13
00:01:11.390 --> 00:01:17.390
Now, I am going to need a listener, but one of the things I want to point out is that I already have a Switchboard listener

14
00:01:17.390 --> 00:01:20.390
and Switchboard shortcut on my desktop.

15
00:01:20.390 --> 00:01:24.390
You want to make sure that when you build these, you have them available and easy to work from.

16
00:01:24.390 --> 00:01:31.579
Now, back into the engine, I'm going to go ahead and launch Switchboard and you'll see my Switchboard user interface pop up.

17
00:01:31.579 --> 00:01:37.579
Now, I'm also going to jump back to the engine and make sure that my Switchboard listener is also launched.

18
00:01:37.579 --> 00:01:41.579
Now, that pops up briefly, but I just want to point out that it looks just like this.

19
00:01:41.579 --> 00:01:47.579
Now, back into Switchboard, I do want to create a new configuration as I already have one available,

20
00:01:47.579 --> 00:01:49.579
but I do want to walk you through each of these steps.

21
00:01:49.579 --> 00:01:54.579
So, go under configurations, which is in the upper left-hand corner of your Switchboard user interface,

22
00:01:54.579 --> 00:01:57.579
and go ahead and hit new configuration.

23
00:01:57.579 --> 00:02:00.579
Now, this is going to work with the path for the project that we've got in here,

24
00:02:00.579 --> 00:02:07.579
and as my Perforce is already checked on and I'm already connected to Perforce, my version control is going to come up that way.

25
00:02:07.579 --> 00:02:12.580
So, this will auto-populate, but you want to make sure that the Perforce path is correct.

26
00:02:12.580 --> 00:02:18.580
So, go ahead and look through here and make sure that your path matches the correct location for your Perforce project.

27
00:02:18.580 --> 00:02:24.580
Also, make sure that the name of the project you're working through is the same as the project that you're actually connecting to.

28
00:02:24.580 --> 00:02:27.580
You'll note here that mine is not correct.

29
00:02:27.580 --> 00:02:29.580
So, I'm going to go ahead and hit detect.

30
00:02:29.580 --> 00:02:32.580
Hitting detect actually hit the correct configuration.

31
00:02:32.580 --> 00:02:37.580
Now, once that is correct, I make sure that my workspace name is correct and hit OK.

32
00:02:37.580 --> 00:02:40.900
Now, I need to actually create my configuration as well.

33
00:02:40.900 --> 00:02:44.900
So, in order to do that, I want to start out with add device.

34
00:02:44.900 --> 00:02:47.900
The first device I'm going to add is the Unreal Engine.

35
00:02:47.900 --> 00:02:50.099
This is my control node.

36
00:02:50.099 --> 00:02:56.099
So, I'm going to type in CTRL node 00 so I know that that's what I'm working from.

37
00:02:56.099 --> 00:03:04.099
Now, I also need to set my IP address for this node, which in this instance is 10.14.101.69.

38
00:03:04.099 --> 00:03:06.219
And I'm going to go ahead and hit OK.

39
00:03:06.219 --> 00:03:13.379
That's going to load in my control node, and if I make a mistake, I can go in and make the adjustments manually here.

40
00:03:13.379 --> 00:03:16.539
Now, I also want to make sure that I'm connecting to my default map.

41
00:03:16.539 --> 00:03:19.539
So, I want to make sure that I'm connecting to my 118 Beach.

42
00:03:19.539 --> 00:03:22.539
Now, I do want to add in another device.

43
00:03:22.539 --> 00:03:25.539
That device is going to be my end display configuration.

44
00:03:25.539 --> 00:03:31.669
Now, as this pops up, you'll see that I've got a pull-down menu that I can't really access right now.

45
00:03:31.669 --> 00:03:38.669
Now, I can browse to it, but in order to get to it quickly, I just hit populate, and that will automatically fill in that location.

46
00:03:38.669 --> 00:03:41.669
And you can see from there, I have a couple of pull-down options.

47
00:03:41.669 --> 00:03:45.830
Now, just to make sure that I'm in the correct location,

48
00:03:45.830 --> 00:03:47.830
I want to hit browse.

49
00:03:47.830 --> 00:03:52.830
That's going to take me to the location, and I want to make sure that I'm in the correct project.

50
00:03:52.830 --> 00:03:54.830
So, again, 118C.

51
00:03:54.830 --> 00:04:01.830
You can see that that matches, and with that connection, I can go ahead and select my NDC asset and hit open.

52
00:04:01.830 --> 00:04:03.830
So, that will give me the correct pathing again.

53
00:04:03.830 --> 00:04:06.860
Once that's done, go ahead and hit OK.

54
00:04:06.860 --> 00:04:12.340
This will auto-populate your multipurpose and render node, or any nodes that you have connected,

55
00:04:12.340 --> 00:04:15.340
along with the correct IP addresses that are in here.

56
00:04:15.340 --> 00:04:22.410
This is pulling these nodes directly from the nDisplay configuration that you've set up previously in the other section.

57
00:04:22.410 --> 00:04:26.410
Now, before I connect here, I do need to make some changes.

58
00:04:26.410 --> 00:04:31.410
You'll notice my multi-user session is not actually correct in here, and I have some settings that I need to cover.

59
00:04:31.410 --> 00:04:37.410
So, I'm going to move up into the top of my switchboard and move my settings, settings option.

60
00:04:37.410 --> 00:04:39.410
Now, I want to make some changes to my multi-user server.

61
00:04:39.410 --> 00:04:45.410
So, I start here, and my server name, you can see, is Stage 11801-503-MUServer.

62
00:04:45.410 --> 00:04:48.410
That's quite a mouthful, and it's a long string of text.

63
00:04:48.410 --> 00:04:53.410
I want to make this a little bit shorter, and if you look at the example in the bottom left-hand corner here,

64
00:04:53.410 --> 00:04:57.410
I want to follow that guideline, but you can set yours to your own naming conventions.

65
00:04:57.410 --> 00:05:04.410
So, I'm going to start with MUS for multi-user session, and I'm going to take out Stage and leave in my 503C.

66
00:05:04.410 --> 00:05:13.540
Then I'm going to replace MUServer here with 118c to match the project name that we're actually using here,

67
00:05:13.540 --> 00:05:15.540
which you can see in this stage path.

68
00:05:15.540 --> 00:05:20.540
Now, I can leave this blank, and I can make that change to my multi-user session,

69
00:05:20.540 --> 00:05:26.540
but I also want to copy this so I can make sure that that exactly matches what I've got here in this session at the bottom.

70
00:05:26.540 --> 00:05:32.660
Next, I want to move down to my end display configuration settings and make sure that my path is correct,

71
00:05:32.660 --> 00:05:37.790
so that I'm going to this 11801c, and then my end display configuration is set.

72
00:05:37.790 --> 00:05:43.790
Next, I want to go to my render node, and you'll note here that my workspace name for my render node says control.

73
00:05:43.790 --> 00:05:49.790
Now, that's incorrect, because my render node has to be on a render machine.

74
00:05:49.790 --> 00:05:59.180
So, that workspace name is going to be changed to RNDR to match the render workspace that we're actually being worked from.

75
00:05:59.180 --> 00:06:03.180
Now, I don't need to make other changes unless I have a completely different path,

76
00:06:03.180 --> 00:06:08.180
but the project for the U project should match stage 11801503CU project,

77
00:06:08.180 --> 00:06:11.180
and you can just see that that actually matches there.

78
00:06:11.180 --> 00:06:18.180
Next, I want to move to the multi-P server and make sure that that workspace name also matches the multi-P.

79
00:06:18.180 --> 00:06:22.180
So, I want to change that to MLTP and make sure that this changes.

80
00:06:22.180 --> 00:06:25.180
Now, you'll note that these both change to gold.

81
00:06:25.180 --> 00:06:30.180
That's just an indicator that there is a change to this auto-populated system for that workspace name,

82
00:06:30.180 --> 00:06:35.589
and it's a good indicator to let you know that your pathing is correct.

83
00:06:35.589 --> 00:06:44.589
Next, I want to move down to my control node and make sure that my workspace name for control matches the workspace for this particular machine.

84
00:06:44.589 --> 00:06:49.589
So, noting that these are correct, I want to go ahead and hit X here to close this window,

85
00:06:49.589 --> 00:06:52.589
and that should save my configuration.

86
00:06:52.589 --> 00:06:57.589
So, again, just hit X, and now I want to make some changes here to this server name.

87
00:06:57.589 --> 00:07:01.009
So, I'm going to paste in the information for my multi-user session,

88
00:07:01.009 --> 00:07:05.009
and I'm going to go ahead and underscore this and start to iterate here.

89
00:07:05.009 --> 00:07:10.009
I'm going to give this the date, so this is 120722 for today's date,

90
00:07:10.009 --> 00:07:15.009
and I'm going to iterate 01 as this is the first time I'm going to join that session.

91
00:07:15.009 --> 00:07:19.009
I'm going to go ahead and hit enter, but before I actually make that connection,

92
00:07:19.009 --> 00:07:25.009
I'm going to go back into my settings and verify that those changes I made actually changed.

93
00:07:25.009 --> 00:07:31.009
Easiest way to do that is to see if these are still gold, and if they are gold, you are golden.

94
00:07:31.009 --> 00:07:38.009
Okay, so we verified our settings for our control nodes, and now we're ready to connect our devices.

95
00:07:38.009 --> 00:07:42.009
First, I'm going to hit this button here, and that will connect all of our Unreal devices,

96
00:07:42.009 --> 00:07:46.009
and next, you'll see that this turns active, so I can actually connect with all of these,

97
00:07:46.009 --> 00:07:49.009
but I just need to connect my nodes for my end display.

98
00:07:49.009 --> 00:07:55.139
Once that's connected, you'll note that I got an error here that says that my render node cannot retrieve my change list.

99
00:07:55.139 --> 00:08:00.139
That's okay, because I've got a sync button here where I can now sync all devices,

100
00:08:00.139 --> 00:08:02.139
so I'm going to go ahead and click that.

101
00:08:02.139 --> 00:08:06.300
Now that I've synchronized all my devices, there's one more thing I need to do.

102
00:08:06.300 --> 00:08:13.389
I need to make sure that I select my map, so I'm going to go from my default map to my beach map.

103
00:08:13.389 --> 00:08:20.389
That's going to make sure that I'm actually launching in the correct project map for my level inside Unreal Engine.

104
00:08:20.389 --> 00:08:28.389
Now, I'm ready to launch, but I don't need this instance, because Switchboard is going to launch a new instance of Unreal Engine.

105
00:08:28.389 --> 00:08:33.389
So from here, I'm just going to switch back to Unreal Engine and go ahead and close this.

106
00:08:33.389 --> 00:08:39.519
Now that that's closed, I've got myself 100% ready to launch the engine here.

107
00:08:39.519 --> 00:08:44.519
So I'm going to go ahead and hit this button here, which is to start all connected devices.

108
00:08:44.519 --> 00:08:53.519
Now, if you're working on a private build, you will also need to build the engine in addition to actually synchronizing everything.

109
00:08:53.519 --> 00:09:01.519
There are some other options in here, so you can build all devices or build and synchronize with your change list immediately after your build is completed.

110
00:09:01.519 --> 00:09:05.519
Okay, so let's go ahead and start all my connected devices.

111
00:09:05.519 --> 00:09:09.320
Now, you'll see this launches my multi-user server.

112
00:09:09.320 --> 00:09:13.320
You'll also see that this is going to launch an instance of Unreal Engine.

113
00:09:13.320 --> 00:09:20.450
As Unreal Engine launches, we'll be able to see what happens on the wall as the engine actually connects.

114
00:09:20.450 --> 00:09:22.450
So let's look at the wall.

115
00:09:22.450 --> 00:09:27.750
Now, you'll see that the wall has gone black.

116
00:09:27.750 --> 00:09:30.750
As the wall goes black, it connects everything to it.

117
00:09:30.750 --> 00:09:39.879
But once it's done, you'll see the engine load and populate and the wall change, and then the inner frustrum pop to fit your camera view.

118
00:09:39.879 --> 00:09:43.879
Now, once that's up and running, you'll see that all of your devices are connected.

119
00:09:43.879 --> 00:09:51.980
And if you switch back to Unreal Engine, you can see that the engine itself has launched the version of the control here.

120
00:09:51.980 --> 00:10:00.980
Now, you'll also see that our multi-user session connects so that you can see my control node, my multi-purpose node, and my render node are connected to my multi-user session.

121
00:10:00.980 --> 00:10:09.230
If I want to make any kind of adjustments, I do want to move down to this bottom right-hand corner and make sure that I've connected to source control.

122
00:10:09.230 --> 00:10:15.649
Once I've connected to source control, you'll notice that the provider says multi-user and not Perforce.

123
00:10:15.649 --> 00:10:22.649
Even though I select Perforce, it's going to retain that multi-user provider because it's connected to all of my devices.

124
00:10:22.649 --> 00:10:27.649
Once that's done, I want to choose my available workspace and accept my settings.

125
00:10:27.649 --> 00:10:31.649
Once connected, you should get a connection successful option.

126
00:10:31.649 --> 00:10:37.740
Now, as you work through your Perforce, you are going to want to make sure that you persist any session changes.

127
00:10:37.740 --> 00:10:41.740
And then once those changes are correct, then you submit your content.

128
00:10:41.740 --> 00:10:46.970
Now, let's jump back to our slides and review what we did for our previous launch.

129
00:10:46.970 --> 00:10:53.970
Now, as we were getting ready to launch, the first thing that we did is make sure that our switchboard listener is connected.

130
00:10:53.970 --> 00:10:58.970
And then from here, we launched both our switchboard listener and our switchboard session.

131
00:10:58.970 --> 00:11:05.970
Again, you do have the option of keeping a few shortcuts on your desktop, which we highly recommend for your production,

132
00:11:05.970 --> 00:11:10.970
so that you can quickly launch your listener and your switchboard instance from those shortcuts.

133
00:11:10.970 --> 00:11:17.000
Now, as you move into your switchboard, you do want to make sure again that your level is correct

134
00:11:17.000 --> 00:11:23.000
and that you then connect your devices first and then change your multi-user session to iterate whatever your session is.

135
00:11:23.000 --> 00:11:30.000
Now, you may only do one multi-user session for a production in the morning and then again in the afternoon.

136
00:11:30.000 --> 00:11:37.100
You might run one session for the entire day, or you might iterate every time you take the wall down and go back up.

137
00:11:37.100 --> 00:11:42.100
It's really up to what you want to use as your user and preference for your production.

138
00:11:42.100 --> 00:11:47.159
Once you're ready to go, again you hit this launch button that you can see in your slides,

139
00:11:47.159 --> 00:11:50.159
and then that will connect all of those devices.

140
00:11:50.159 --> 00:11:56.159
In order to take this down, that send icon will change to a stop X icon after launch.

141
00:11:56.159 --> 00:12:04.320
Now that you're ready to go, you have already connected to switchboard through the wall and transacted to the LED volume.
